# media.xcl - find what a page offers and download it.
#
# Three verbs do the work here:
#
#   media                    list the page's media URLs as JSON
#   download <url> <path>    save a direct file, an HLS (.m3u8) playlist, or a
#                            DASH (.mpd) manifest - assembled natively
#   capture <url> <path> [s] open a page, let it play, and reassemble the media
#                            it fetches itself (MSE: YouTube, Facebook)
#
# To *look* at a page (the indexed snapshot with images/video and hover
# previews), run an XCL script that calls the plugin's `snapshot` op (the
# interactive session that used to expose `snapshot` has been removed - the CLI
# is now just a script runner):
#
#   import browser
#   run browser snapshot
#
# Run this script:
#   xcelerate run media.xcl --verbose
#
# Every path below is confined to the workspace root (the directory you run from,
# or `--output-dir <dir>`): absolute paths and `..` escapes are refused, so a
# script can never write outside the workspace you chose.

param base  "https://www.python.org"
param image "https://www.python.org/static/img/python-logo.png"

open $base
wait-idle

# 1. Ask for the media URLs (JSON, absolute):
#      {"count":1,"media":[{"kind":"image","url":".../python-logo.png",...}]}
media

# 2. Save a direct file. `download` runs the request through the browser, so the
#    page's cookies apply and CORS never blocks it. It also assembles a segmented
#    stream, so a playlist URL "just downloads":
download $image "media-out/logo.png"
#   download "https://test-streams.mux.dev/x36xhzz/x36xhzz.m3u8" "media-out/movie.ts"   # HLS
#   download "https://.../manifest.mpd" "media-out/movie.mp4"                            # DASH

# 3. A page that plays through Media Source Extensions has no file URL - point
#    `capture` at the page and it records the segments the player fetches:
#   capture "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=..." "media-out/video.mp4" 20

done
